1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,472
The Curiosity Club.

2
00:00:02,472 --> 00:00:04,714
Episode 30.

3
00:00:04,714 --> 00:00:07,554
The Mountain with a Secret Door.

4
00:00:07,554 --> 00:00:10,448
Here is something to listen for.

5
00:00:10,448 --> 00:00:14,334
What do we call magma after it reaches the surface?

6
00:00:14,334 --> 00:00:15,855
Ready?

7
00:00:15,855 --> 00:00:17,961
Let us find out.

8
00:00:17,961 --> 00:00:22,961
At a science center, Leo sees a model volcano cut in half.

9
00:00:22,961 --> 00:00:27,336
Tubes and colored sections show what may lie beneath the surface.

10
00:00:27,336 --> 00:00:30,570
It has secret passages, he says.

11
00:00:30,570 --> 00:00:33,274
Is this where a dragon lives?

12
00:00:33,274 --> 00:00:35,679
Pip searches the label.

13
00:00:35,679 --> 00:00:38,682
No dragon residence is listed.

14
00:00:38,682 --> 00:00:43,926
The educator explains that molten rock below Earth's surface is called magma.

15
00:00:43,926 --> 00:00:47,744
When it erupts onto the surface, it is called lava.

16
00:00:47,744 --> 00:00:53,546
Same journey, different location, Maya says, writing the two names beside a drawing.

17
00:00:53,546 --> 00:00:57,730
The educator shows photographs of different volcanoes.

18
00:00:57,730 --> 00:01:00,747
Some are broad with gentle slopes.

19
00:01:00,747 --> 00:01:03,057
Others are steeper.

20
00:01:03,057 --> 00:01:06,426
Eruptions can also look very different.

21
00:01:06,426 --> 00:01:11,317
So our one model is not a picture of every volcano, Leo says.

22
00:01:11,317 --> 00:01:13,002
Correct.

23
00:01:13,002 --> 00:01:18,084
Models help us understand selected features, but real systems vary.

24
00:01:18,084 --> 00:01:21,345
They look at a map of Earth's moving plates.

25
00:01:21,345 --> 00:01:27,785
Many volcanoes occur near plate boundaries, but some form away from those boundaries at hot spots.

26
00:01:27,785 --> 00:01:31,032
Pip wants to put a doorbell on the model.

27
00:01:31,032 --> 00:01:34,728
It could warn the mountain that visitors have arrived.

28
00:01:34,728 --> 00:01:40,230
Scientists use instruments and observations instead, says the educator.

29
00:01:40,230 --> 00:01:45,489
They study signals such as small earthquakes, changes in shape, and gases.

30
00:01:45,489 --> 00:01:54,089
Maya names their drawing The Mountain with a Secret Door, then adds a clear subtitle: An imaginary title for a real geological process.

31
00:01:54,089 --> 00:01:58,505
Leo draws the dragon in a separate corner labeled Fiction.

32
00:01:58,505 --> 00:02:00,434
Pip approves.

33
00:02:00,434 --> 00:02:03,953
The dragon finally has a correct address.

34
00:02:03,953 --> 00:02:06,970
Now, a little more to discover.

35
00:02:06,970 --> 00:02:13,600
Magma is molten rock beneath Earth's surface; lava is molten rock after it reaches the surface.

36
00:02:13,600 --> 00:02:19,497
Many volcanoes occur at tectonic plate boundaries, and others occur at hot spots.

37
00:02:19,497 --> 00:02:25,040
Scientists monitor several kinds of evidence to study volcanic activity.

38
00:02:25,040 --> 00:02:29,551
Volcanoes do not all have the same shape or eruption style.

39
00:02:29,551 --> 00:02:32,581
Let us hear the main idea again.

40
00:02:32,581 --> 00:02:38,206
A cutaway model helped explain the path from underground magma to surface lava.

41
00:02:38,206 --> 00:02:44,633
The club compared different volcanoes and learned that one simple model cannot represent every detail.

42
00:02:44,633 --> 00:02:47,635
Here are three useful expressions.

43
00:02:47,635 --> 00:02:50,679
You can listen, or say them with me.

44
00:02:50,679 --> 00:02:53,641
What is happening below the surface?

45
00:02:53,641 --> 00:02:56,603
What is happening below the surface?

46
00:02:56,603 --> 00:02:59,239
It has a different name here.

47
00:02:59,239 --> 00:03:01,874
It has a different name here.

48
00:03:01,874 --> 00:03:05,312
This model shows only part of the story.

49
00:03:05,312 --> 00:03:08,749
This model shows only part of the story.

50
00:03:08,749 --> 00:03:11,616
Remember our listening question?

51
00:03:11,616 --> 00:03:15,502
What do we call magma after it reaches the surface?

52
00:03:15,502 --> 00:03:17,690
Here is the answer.

53
00:03:17,690 --> 00:03:19,836
We call it lava.

54
00:03:19,836 --> 00:03:23,627
And here is a question just for your imagination.

55
00:03:23,627 --> 00:03:29,388
If you could see a cutaway view of one ordinary object, which object would you choose?

56
00:03:29,388 --> 00:03:32,554
There is more than one possible answer.

57
00:03:32,554 --> 00:03:35,706
Keep wondering, and see you next time.
